-- title:
--   Town balls are finite
-- statement:
--   For real parameters $h$ (the town scale) and $\ell$ (the block size), a centre
--   $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and a radius $R \in \mathbb{R}$, write $\|x\|_\infty = \max_i|x_i|$, let
--
--   $$Q_\ell(x) \;=\; \mathbb{Z}^d \cap \{\, y : \|y-x\|_\infty \le \ell/2 \,\} \qquad (\texttt{block } \ell\ x)$$
--
--   be the block of size $\ell$ based at $x$, and let the *town ball* be the collection of blocks whose
--   lattice index lies within $R$ of $z$:
--
--   $$\mathrm{TB}(h,\ell;z,R) \;=\; \big\{\, Q_\ell(h\,p) \;:\; p \in \mathbb{Z}^d,\ \lVert p - z\rVert < R \,\big\}
--   \qquad (\texttt{townBall } h\ \ell\ z\ R),$$
--
--   a set of *subsets* of $\mathbb{R}^d$ (it is the index $p$, not the block $Q_\ell(hp)$ itself, that
--   is constrained to lie within $R$ of $z$). Then
--
--   $$\mathrm{TB}(h,\ell;z,R) \text{ is a finite set.}$$
--
--   Finiteness is what makes the town ball usable as the vertex set of a combinatorial argument: a walk
--   confined to a finite vertex set can be shortened to a path whose length is bounded by the size of
--   that set, and every length bound in the chaining construction comes from such a bound.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypotheses are imposed on $h$, $\ell$ or $R$ — they are arbitrary reals,
--   negative and zero values included — so the statement covers the degenerate parameter ranges as
--   well, where the town ball is empty or otherwise trivial.
